13| *
14| * Issue #847: `task_id` was written straight from the client into
15| * `tickets.task_id`, with no validation of any kind. `unit_id` was already
16| * gated by `TicketTargetUnit` (#818), so `task_id` was the last input on the
17| * form without even an existence check — which meant a missing id raised
18| * `QueryException SQLSTATE[23503]` instead of a validation message, and any
19| * real id linked the ticket to a todo in a unit the creator cannot reach. That
20| * link then leaked the todo's title through three read surfaces and let a
21| * `unit_manager` force `is_completed = true` on it.
22| *
23| * The unit question (the product decision recorded on #847, option ج):
24| * `tickets.task_id` must point at a todo inside the **creator's** scope —
25| * `AccessService::accessibleUnitIds()` — and not at the destination unit.
26| * Unlike `unit_id`, the related task is not routed to the destination; an
27| * explicit pick yields the creator's unit, and only the auto-create branch
28| * (see the component) targets the destination.
29| *
30| * `is_completed = false` is part of the rule, not an extra nicety: the picker
31| * only ever offered open todos, so accepting a completed one was always a
32| * server-side/UI disagreement, and a completed task would immediately satisfy
33| * the "all tickets of this task are done" condition in `submitAction()`.
34| *
35| * A **null-unit** todo is treated as in-scope when it belongs to the actor,
36| * which is the creator-ownership contract of issue #838.
37| *
38| * The scope predicate is an unconditional `whereIn` on purpose: an empty scope
39| * must compile to `0 = 1` and fail closed. Never guard it with
40| * `->when($ids, fn ($q) => $q->whereIn(...))` — an empty array is falsy, the
41| * callback is skipped, and the row is accepted (the #819 leak).
42| */
